@* Principle 2 – Captions and other alternatives for multimedia *@
@{
    var hasVideo = ViewData["HasVideo"] is true;
}

<h2>Know the alternatives</h2>
<dl>
    <dt><dfn>Captions</dfn></dt>
    <dd>Text of the speech <em>and</em> important sounds (for example “[audience laughs]”), synchronized with the video.
        For people who are Deaf or hard of hearing, or anyone watching with the sound off.</dd>

    <dt><dfn>Transcript</dfn></dt>
    <dd>All of the audio as text on the page. Required for audio-only content like podcasts. Transcripts are also searchable
        and help people who read faster than they listen.</dd>

    <dt><dfn>Audio description</dfn></dt>
    <dd>Narration that describes important visual details (“She points to the error on the screen”) for people who are blind
        or have low vision.</dd>

    <dt><dfn>Sign language interpretation</dfn></dt>
    <dd>A signer shown alongside the video. For many Deaf people sign language is their first language, so it can be
        easier to follow than captions.</dd>
</dl>

<h2>Captioned video player</h2>
@if (hasVideo)
{
    <p>Use the player's <strong>CC</strong> menu to turn captions or descriptions on and off. Nothing plays until you press play.</p>
    <div class="video-wrap">
        @* No autoplay. The <track> elements add captions and text descriptions. *@
        <video controls preload="metadata" aria-describedby="video-desc">
            <source src="~/media/sample.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
            <track kind="captions" src="~/media/sample.en.vtt" srclang="en" label="English captions" default />
            <track kind="descriptions" src="~/media/sample.descriptions.en.vtt" srclang="en" label="English audio descriptions" />
            <p>Your browser cannot play this video. <a href="~/media/sample.mp4">Download the video</a> or read the transcript below.</p>
        </video>
        <p id="video-desc">Video: an interview about why accessibility matters. A transcript follows.</p>
    </div>
}
else
{
    <div class="callout">
        <p><strong>No sample video is installed yet.</strong> To see a working captioned player, copy any short MP4 file to
            <code>wwwroot/media/sample.mp4</code> and reload this page. The caption file
            <code>wwwroot/media/sample.en.vtt</code> is already included; edit its timings to match your video.</p>
    </div>
}

<p>This is the markup the player uses:</p>
<code-sample label="Video with captions and descriptions">
    <video controls preload="metadata">
        <source src="sample.mp4" type="video/mp4">
        <track kind="captions" src="sample.en.vtt" srclang="en" label="English captions" default>
        <track kind="descriptions" src="sample.descriptions.en.vtt" srclang="en" label="English audio descriptions">
    </video>
</code-sample>

<p>Captions are stored in a <abbr title="Web Video Text Tracks">WebVTT</abbr> file, which is plain text:</p>
<code-sample label="WebVTT caption file">
    WEBVTT

    00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
    <v Host>Welcome back to Tech Talk. Today we're talking about accessibility.</v>

    00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.500
    [upbeat music fades]
</code-sample>

<h2>Transcript example</h2>
<p>
    A transcript should identify speakers and include meaningful sounds. Putting it in a
    <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> element keeps the page short while still making it easy to find.
</p>
<details>
    <summary>Read the transcript: “Why accessibility matters” radio interview (about 2 minutes)</summary>
    <div>
        <p>[Upbeat theme music, then fades]</p>
        <p><strong>Host:</strong> Welcome back to Tech Talk. Today we're talking about web accessibility with a software developer who tests websites for a living.</p>
        <p><strong>Guest:</strong> Thanks for having me. Accessibility means people with disabilities can perceive, understand, navigate, and use the web.</p>
        <p><strong>Host:</strong> Who benefits?</p>
        <p><strong>Guest:</strong> Around one in four adults in the U.S. has some type of disability. But captions also help someone watching on a noisy bus, and keyboard support helps a power user who never touches a mouse.</p>
        <p><strong>Host:</strong> [laughs] I'm guilty of that one.</p>
        <p><strong>Guest:</strong> Most of us are! My advice to students is to test with a keyboard and a screen reader early. It's much cheaper than fixing problems at the end.</p>
        <p><strong>Host:</strong> Great advice. Thanks for joining us.</p>
        <p>[Theme music returns]</p>
    </div>
</details>

<h2>Sign language interpretation</h2>
<p>
    Sign language is usually shown as a picture-in-picture video or a second video beside the main one.
    It is a requirement at WCAG level AAA, and organizations that serve Deaf communities often provide it.
    If you add one, give the interpreter's video enough size and contrast to see hand shapes and facial expressions clearly.
</p>

<h2>Don't</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Don't rely on auto-generated captions without checking them; they often get names and technical terms wrong.</li>
    <li>Don't autoplay media with sound. (See <a asp-action="Distinguishable">easier to see and hear</a>.)</li>
    <li>Don't put captions only inside the video image (“burned-in” or open captions) if you can provide a text track instead; text tracks can be resized and restyled.</li>
</ul>
